  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nA low-level jet over the region is the driver behind both scattered \nrain showers and gusty southerly winds this afternoon. Expectations \nfor the rest of the day are that showers will continue to develop \nand move slowly eastward through the rest of today and through the \novernight period. The vast majority of these showers will have very \nlight rain rates this afternoon, although some higher rain rates and \na stray rumble of thunder or two may be possible in the strongest \nshowers. As the low-level jet moves eastward tonight, more \nwidespread development will take place. Stronger rain rates are \nexpected in this development, and around a quarter of an inch will \nfall along and east of the James River (60-90% chance from the NBM). \nPerhaps up to a half inch or more may fall through Thursday \nafternoon, beyond which rain is expected to be out of the area. The \nother impact of the low-level jet's progression to the east is that \ngusty winds up to 30-35 miles per hour are expected to persist over \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota overnight tonight \nand into early Thursday morning. \n\nAn upper-level trough based over the Pacific Northwest will eject a \nshortwave into the region late Friday into Saturday, bringing about \nthe next chances for rain. Once again the best chances for rain will \nbe during the overnight period. Through Saturday morning, chances to \nsee a quarter of an inch sit at around 30-60%, with the highest \nlocated along the James River. There is still a fair degree of \nspread with this system however, and amounts as high as half an inch \nto even an inch of rain through Saturday morning are very much in \nplay. This system is expected to exit by Saturday afternoon. \n\nEarly next week, another upper-level trough over the Pacific will \nprogress eastward to be over the western CONUS while deepening. This \ntrough will develop a low pressure center that will move into the \nNorthern Great Plains, bringing about additional chances for rain \ninto the region. There is still a bit of uncertainty in the timing \nof this rain, but best chances are currently Monday afternoon \nthrough overnight. Current ensemble medians highlight eastern South \nDakota and Western Minnesota for the area of highest rainfall \ntotals, and ensemble medians in that area sit anywhere between 0.5\"-\n1.5\" through Wednesday morning. NBM probability to see 1\" total over \nthat same Monday-Wednesday time period peaks at 50-70% along and \neast of the James River. Mid- to long-range machine learning models \ndo hint at some severe potential with this system on Monday, so that \nwill be something to keep an eye on as this event draws closer.",
